# Secure DNS for Beginners

This page explains how to improve your home network security and privacy by changing your DNS settings.

If you are using the Browser Leak Tool, your browser will follow the DNS servers configured on your system or router. That means DNS settings can affect privacy, malware blocking, and content filtering across your network.

This guide is designed for beginners, so everything is explained in simple terms.

---

## What Is DNS?

DNS means Domain Name System.

It is the system that turns website names like:
- google.com
- youtube.com
- example.com

into IP addresses that computers understand.

Without DNS, you would have to memorize number addresses instead of website names.

Example:
- DNS: google.com → 142.250.190.78
- Browser: “I want to open google.com”

So DNS is like the internet’s address book.

---

## Why Should I Change My DNS?

Most home routers use the DNS provided by your Internet Service Provider (ISP).

That is often:
- slower
- less private
- less secure
- not as good at blocking harmful websites

Using a trusted DNS provider can help with:
- privacy
- faster browsing
- malware blocking
- family filtering
- ad/tracker blocking

